      "math_academy_id": "q-99019",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials",
      "problem": "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+12x+13.$",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+12x+13) \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}+24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+24x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+8x+4 & =0 \\\\ (3x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2.$",
