      "math_academy_id": "q-344867",
      "knowledge_point_id": "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions",
      "problem": "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function  $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot (2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+8-(2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-2x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So, $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are \n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=2.\n$$",
