      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$\n\n- setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (0,2\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
