    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-132569",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{2})+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈1.292\\,50$ rounded to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-1.849\\,10$ rounded to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx≈1.292\\,50,\\,x≈-1.849\\,10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-1.849\\,10$ and $x≈1.292\\,50.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}≈-1.849\\,10$ and ${x}_{2}≈1.292\\,50.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-1.849\\,10\\cdot 1.292\\,50≈-2.39\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places",
