  "canonical_examples": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6722",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials",
      "problem": "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2x^3-3x^2+5.$",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ 6x(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x=0,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=0$ and $x=1.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6723",
      "knowledge_point_id": "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions",
      "problem": "Find the critical points of the function $f(x) = e^{x^3-x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{x^3-x} \\neq 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by $e^{x^3-x}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.$",
      "difficulty": null,
